Looking at this problem, I need to count the number of squares where I can place a piece without it being captured by existing pieces.

A piece at position (i,j) can capture any piece on:
- The same row i
- The same column j  
- The same diagonal where i+j is constant
- The same anti-diagonal where i-j is constant

So I need to count squares that are NOT on any of these lines for any existing piece.

Let me use the inclusion-exclusion principle to count attacked squares, then subtract from N².

For each existing piece, I'll track:
- rows_used: all rows that have pieces
- cols_used: all columns that have pieces
- diag_used: all diagonal sums (i+j values) that have pieces
- anti_diag_used: all anti-diagonal differences (i-j values) that have pieces

Then I'll count:
- R: squares in used rows
- C: squares in used columns  
- D: squares on used diagonals
- A: squares on used anti-diagonals

And apply inclusion-exclusion to avoid double-counting overlaps.

